================================================================================================
GATE 1 -- is the rank-40 defect subspace a stable target?
================================================================================================

object        n_patch    k     same-obj (mean+-sd)  random floor
wallplugs         284   40           0.746 +- 0.010         0.104
sheet_metal      1747   40           0.918 +- 0.008         0.104
vial             2160   40           0.915 +- 0.007         0.104
can                27   12           0.520 +- 0.030         0.031

  random floor = k/d (what two unrelated rank-k subspaces score)

pair (cross-object)            overlap  random floor
wallplugs vs sheet_metal         0.300         0.104
wallplugs vs vial                0.285         0.104
wallplugs vs can                 0.117         0.031
sheet_metal vs vial              0.297         0.104
sheet_metal vs can               0.188         0.031
vial vs can                      0.118         0.031

  mean cross-object overlap = 0.218

  VERDICT: same-object must clearly exceed BOTH the random floor and cross-object.
           If it does not, there is no stable target to estimate.

================================================================================================
GATE 2 -- where does the defect subspace sit in the NORMAL eigenspectrum?
================================================================================================

  enrichment = (defect energy share in a normal-PCA window) / (window's share of dims)
  1.0 = indifferent to normal geometry (a random subspace would score ~1.0)

object          k    PCA[0:20]   PCA[20:50]  PCA[50:100] PCA[100:200] PCA[200:384]  |  max enr
wallplugs      40         7.35         4.05         1.06         0.38         0.13        7.35
sheet_metal    40        13.14         1.32         0.88         0.26         0.06       13.14
vial           40         3.24         4.27         2.13         0.52         0.17        4.27
can            12         7.32         2.70         1.64         0.42         0.18        7.32

  Control: a RANDOM rank-k subspace scores ~1.0 in every window.
  Enrichment concentrated in the low-variance tail (rightmost windows) would
  mean normal geometry does constrain the defect direction.
  Flat ~1.0 everywhere means normal covariance carries NO signal about it.
